# The build context of deploy/Dockerfile, the API's release image, which is built from the
# repository root (`podman build -f deploy/Dockerfile .`). The Dockerfile copies this whole
# narrowed context into its builder stage.
#
# Everything is excluded, then the build's inputs are re-included. Cargo loads every workspace
# member before it builds one, and `cargo build --locked` holds only when it resolves the member
# set Cargo.lock records, so the workspace manifest, the lock file and the folders that hold the
# members (tools, crates) come along, beside the two contract folders whose files the API embeds
# with `include_str!` (the JSON Schemas of contracts/definitions, and contracts/rules:
# kit-aliases.json and equipment-gameplay/native-matching.json). The Enfusion mod at `mod/` holds
# no member and no file a Rust build reads, so it stays out with everything else. An exception
# names a folder literally: the container builders skip an excluded folder unless an exception
# starts with its path, so a wildcard exception such as `!crates/*/Cargo.toml` re-includes nothing.
*
!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!tools
!crates
!contracts/definitions
!contracts/rules

# Inside those folders, what no Rust build reads: build output, installed npm packages, and the
# local secrets files (`crates/api/api_server/.env`), which must never reach an image layer.
**/target
**/dist
**/node_modules
**/.env
**/.env.*
